{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 333 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "425813e2a0d4f5c1c9540131ce491f91487c7f4b4c7c6356f2d7a26e3900227d" :edb/db-before-t 332 :edb/db-after-t 333 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533645 50 #inst "2026-10-04T05:56:26.791Z" 13194139533645 true] [17592186068758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243797 13194139533645 true] [17592186068758 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243818 13194139533645 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243804 13194139533645 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243811 13194139533645 true] [17592186069363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533645 true] [17592186069363 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ hence $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-3 & =3 \\\\ k-3 & =3 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=6-(-3)=9.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=6{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3.$" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243797 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243798 13194139533645 true] [17592186243797 1089 "q-170349" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243797 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533645 true] [17592186243797 1100 "For the curve $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139533645 true] [17592186243797 1113 #uuid "d4172d49-9de6-50d7-a291-6f9452de375f" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243797 1138 false 13194139533645 true] [17592186243797 1145 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{1}{2}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243799 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243800 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243801 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243802 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243803 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1041 "selection" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243803 13194139533645 true] [17592186243798 1110 #uuid "1ec24b9b-d970-5b5f-9636-4936c9b671f8" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243799 1034 "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533645 true] [17592186243799 1111 #uuid "1f94b193-985e-5fa4-93e9-cbbbb376f300" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243800 1034 "(0,\\frac{13}{10})" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243800 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533645 true] [17592186243800 1111 #uuid "7bbc8c57-260f-5e69-a8d9-816990a3462e" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243801 1034 "(0,1)" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533645 true] [17592186243801 1111 #uuid "333e2172-7ae1-5a82-bf98-851d5991c9a6" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243802 1034 "(0,\\frac{21}{10})" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243802 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533645 true] [17592186243802 1111 #uuid "1eb2a3c9-b515-566c-abff-cd0e276887f5" 13194139533645 true] [17592186243803 1034 "(0,\\frac{5}{2})" 13194139533645 tru